            <ArticleTitle>Case report: bilateral slipped capital femoral epiphyses and hormone replacement.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>743-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s11999-007-0099-x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 24-year-old woman presented with an 11-year history of bilateral hip pain. Radiographs of the hips revealed severe bilateral slipped upper femoral epiphyses; the left side was more severely slipped than the right. While moving the hips under fluoroscopy we observed motion at the physes and reproduced the patient's pain; the motion confirmed the diagnosis of chronic slipped capital femoral epiphysis. Endocrinology tests showed hypothyroidism. After 1 year of thyroxin therapy, the patient's pain subsided and radiographs of the hips showed fusion of the physes. This case emphasizes the importance of screening for an endocrine disorder in patients with slipped capital femoral epiphysis particularly in adults and shows fusion can occur once the underlying endocrine abnormality is treated.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
